WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.275
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We covered
monitoring GKE clusters

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:03.990
all the way back in
our first episode.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.240
But there are lots of new
features in Cloud Operations

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.370
to help you observe and
manage your GKE clusters

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.920
and the workloads
that run on them.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.925
Today, I'm going to show you how
to use Cloud Operations for GKE

00:00:12.925 --> 00:00:14.550
to make sure your
services are healthy.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.930
This is a Stack Doctor.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.332
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.138 --> 00:00:25.180
Right away you'll see that
the new GKE Monitoring

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.000
Dashboard looks very different.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.600
Instead of three
tabs, you now have

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.330
lists of all the different
GKE entities, like clusters,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:32.800
namespaces, and nodes.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.750
Remember, this
information is aggregated

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.330
from all the projects being
monitored by your Workspace.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.580
This gives you a single place
to see everything you're

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:40.780
responsible for managing.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.730
Now right now, you might be
thinking, well, that's great,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:44.740
but I have a default
namespace in every cluster.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.465
How will I ever find
out what I need?

00:00:46.465 --> 00:00:48.340
Well, there's a really
cool filtering feature

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:49.780
that will help you to
find exactly what you're

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:50.420
looking for.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.420
For example, if you have
a copy of the front end

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.370
service running in three
different clusters,

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.960
the filter lets you select
the exact one you want.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.510
Once you apply the
filter, the entire

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:01.043
dashboard its filtered to match.

00:01:01.043 --> 00:01:03.210
One of the things that's
been hard to do in the past

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.183
is to get an aggregated
view of the data.

00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:07.350
For example, what does my
resource utilization looks

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.150
like across my namespace?

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:10.830
This new view makes
it really easy

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.180
to get this kind of aggregation.

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.100
And it'll scale no
matter how many resources

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.710
are being monitored, even if
you have thousands of pods

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.930
across hundreds of namespaces.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.090
But if you do want to see
all of those resources,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.850
you can click View All.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.615
That will let you see all of
the entities in that category.

00:01:26.615 --> 00:01:27.990
If that list is
too overwhelming,

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.970
you can filter from here, too.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.010
At any point, you can
select a row in the table

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.000
to get its details.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.970
For example, click a pod
to open its detailed panel.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.980
This view has everything
you need in one place--

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.990
metadata about the
resource, any incidence

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.600
that it has triggered, and
metric and log data, too.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.000
Having a single place to
access all this information

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.760
makes understanding what's
going on much easier.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.360
On the Metrics
tab, you can easily

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.360
select a smaller subset
of time if you're trying

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.130
to zero in on an anomaly.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.260
Just drag the rectangle
on the chart that includes

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.140
the time you want to focus on.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.640
From any Metric chart, you can
then create a learning policy

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.760
by clicking on that link.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.260
This is a great way to quickly
get started with the learning

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.700
GKE entities right in context.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.160
You can also get right to the
logs of the resource you've

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.000
selected.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.338
For example, select a pod
and click on the Logs tab

00:02:11.338 --> 00:02:12.796
to see the most
recent logs for it.

00:02:12.796 --> 00:02:15.813
If you're troubleshooting an
issue, you can filter the logs.

00:02:15.813 --> 00:02:18.480
For example, you might only want
to see error logs to figure out

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.950
why a pod is crashing.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:23.460
To get more details, click on
the Open and Logging button.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.990
That will open the
new Logs Viewer

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.247
with your query
pre-populated and executed.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:28.830
This really makes
it very easy to know

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.750
exactly what's going
on with your workloads

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.333
without having to figure out how
to select the resource you're

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:34.047
looking for.

00:02:34.047 --> 00:02:35.880
You may recall that we
covered using logging

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.380
with your GKE
clusters and workloads

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.797
in an earlier episode.

00:02:38.797 --> 00:02:41.130
We'll link to that below if
you haven't seen it already.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.520
Thanks for joining me today.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.730
We took a look at the new
GKE Monitoring Dashboard

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.160
and learned how to use it
in-context capabilities

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.988
to set up a learning and pull
up logs for our workload.

00:02:49.988 --> 00:02:52.030
Thanks for watching, and
come back soon for more.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.070
This is a Stack Doctor.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.090
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:57.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

